-- title:
--   Generation in degree m for points of the Hilbert functor
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $n, m$ and a function $h : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ subject to two hypotheses: first, $h(d) = \binom{n+d}{n}$ for every $d < m$; second, for every $e \ge m$, every field $K$ and every ideal $J$ of the polynomial ring $K[x_0,\dots,x_n] =$ `MvPolynomial (Fin (n+1)) K` which admits a generating set consisting of forms of degree $e$, if the $K$-dimension of `piece J e` — the space of degree-$e$ forms modulo those lying in $J$, i.e. the degree-$e$ graded piece of $K[x]/J$ — equals $h(e)$, then the dimension of `piece J (e+1)` is at most $h(e+1)$. Let $A$ be a commutative ring and let $P$ be a point of the Hilbert functor over $A$ with Hilbert function $h$, that is, a structure `Point A n h` consisting of an ideal $I =$ `P.I` of $A[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ containing all homogeneous components of each of its elements, such that for every $d$ the $A$-module `piece I d` is finite and projective and has rank $h(d)$ at every prime of $A$. The conclusion is that $I$ coincides with the ideal generated by the set of those $p \in I$ that are homogeneous of degree $m$.
--
--   This is the Gotzmann-type persistence statement underlying the representability of the Hilbert functor: in the range where the Hilbert function is maximal below degree $m$ and satisfies a Macaulay growth bound from $m$ on, a flat family of homogeneous ideals with that Hilbert function is generated by its degree-$m$ forms. It is used in the construction of the closed immersion cutting out the Hilbert scheme inside a Grassmannian, via the statements describing when a polynomial belongs to the ideal of a point.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_HilbertFunctor_Point_I_eq_span_of_forall_finrank_piece_succ_le.lean

